on":2024,"team":"JAX","distance":0.5201,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"CAR","distance":0.5207,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Joe Brady"}],"defense":[{"season":2019,"team":"MIA","distance":0.8349,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Patrick Graham"},{"season":2023,"team":"NO","distance":0.8682,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2021,"team":"NO","distance":0.8706,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2018,"team":"KC","distance":0.9068,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"NO","distance":0.9261,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Lions went 3-12-1 in 2019 and were outscored 423 to 341. The offense ranked 21st in EPA per play and the defense 25th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. Their most distinctive trait was the 2nd highest Cover 1 rate in the league.","offense":"The Lions offense was a little below average by EPA per play, and it was much better passing the ball than running it. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Matthew Stafford ran the passing game, with Kenny Golladay leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Lions defense was well below average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 15th and 26th. Devon Kennard was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Trey Flowers, who had 7 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd highest Cover 1 rate in the league.","special":"The Lions were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 14% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 84% of field goals, which was a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 15% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 42% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Matthew Stafford played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,499 yards. Targets were spread around, with Kenny Golladay and Danny Amendola leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Kerryon Johnson and Bo Scarbrough. On defense, Devon Kennard led the team with 7 sacks and Darius Slay picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Titans and the 2023 Jets. Their defense looked most like the 2019 Dolphins under Patrick Graham and the 2023 Saints under Dennis Allen.","trends":"The offense went from 23rd in EPA per play in 2018 to 21st in 2019, about where it was. The defense moved from 21st in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 25th in 2019, a modest decline. The biggest change in approach was empty formation usage, which went from 4% to 11%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}